  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 211 AM EDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\n- Northwest flow regime with occasional chances for rain but low\n  confidence in convective details\n\nThe Great Lakes region remains in between an upper high across \nthe western CONUS and longwave troughing across the eastern CONUS \nwith occasional shortwave troughs passing through and bringing \nthe chance for some much-needed rain.\n\nA weakening shortwave trough moving through early this morning\nhas yet to initiate any showers, which is not surprising given\nlimited moisture and lift. Isolated showers are possible through\nthe afternoon.\n\nStill low confidence in the details of how things play out tonight\nthrough MOnday night in terms of organized convection, but \ninstability and shear are sufficient for a slow-moving front and \nweak midlevel vorticity max to focus convection across Minnesota \nand into Wisconsin this afternoon which eventually moves southeast\nacross Lake Michigan and perhaps western Lower Michigan. \n\nThe hi-res convective allowing models take the bulk of the storms\nwest of our forecast area, but there is a chance an MCS could\nimpinge on the western or southwest zones late tonight or early\nMonday morning. Additional convection is possible across the rest\nof the forecast area during Monday and Monday night as the front \npushes south.\n\nThe front moves through by Tuesday with fair weather for mid to \nlate week before the longwave upper trough amplifies with a cold \nfront bringing another chance of rain by next weekend.",
